-- #1111 A-2.6: the SIBLING-DECL route, and the counter-example to the claim that a
-- member which looks like a type name "contributes nothing".
--
-- Rule (b) of `importedCtorTypeDecls` is a CONSTRUCTOR-name test.  `pmod` declares
-- no type `T` at all — only `data Wrap = T Int` — so `import pmod.{T, pf}` matches
-- by rule (b) and contributes `Wrap`, sibling constructors and all.  A member
-- spelled like a type therefore DOES contribute whenever the module has some other
-- decl with a constructor of that spelling.  `ctor_scope_type_only_no_hijack` is
-- green only because `hja`'s single variant is `Hit`, i.e. because of its shape —
-- not because type-shaped members are inert.
--
-- What keeps that harmless is the LAYERING, not the rule: the overlay is registered
-- below this module's own `prog0`, so `Wrap`'s `T` cannot clobber the `T` declared
-- right here.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE any capture and independently of any
-- engine: `T "hi"` is `Loc`'s `T` (this module's own declaration wins), `unloc`
-- gives `hi`; `pf 5` is `5`.
--
-- ⚠️ THE FAILURE THIS PINS, measured on the first cut of A-2.6 (PR #1282 as first
-- pushed, binary built from f6c2d391): REJECTED, `Type mismatch: Wrap vs Loc`, exit
-- 1, FIVE diagnostics.  Pristine `main` (26585d3c) exits 0 and prints `hi`/`5`.
--
-- ⚠️ Control, measured on BOTH binaries: change the import to `pmod.{pf}` and it is
-- green either way — so it is the member `T`, not the mere presence of `Wrap`, that
-- drives it.  Deleting `Wrap` is NOT a usable control: it also un-exports the name
-- `T`, so the import fails at RESOLVE with `Module 'pmod' has no exported name 'T'`.
import pmod.{T, pf}

data Loc = T String

unloc : Loc -> String
unloc (T s) = s

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (unloc (T "hi"))
  putStrLn (intToString (pf 5))
